## Introduction

In the southernmost regions of its distribution, like in the northern taga of European Russia, permafrost occurrence is restricted to mires. Typical permafrost landforms are palsals and peat plateaus (Andreev, 1935; P'yavchenko, 1955; Oksanen, 2002). These landforms consist of peat/mineral soil and segregated ice. Palsas are usually 1-7 m high and a few metres to 100 m in diameter. Peat plateaus are larger, flat-topped elevated expanses of peat (van Everdingen, 1998). Aqua ridges can in some cases be considered permafrost landforms as well, maintaining frost over several years and longer (string palsas). Permatrost degradation in mires is most noticeably observed as collapse scars and thermokarst ponds.

Through its effect on topography and hydrology, permafrost is significant for the ecology and carbon balance of mires. An estimated 22 % of the carbon pool in mires of the former Soviet Union is located in permafrost mires (Botch _et al.,_ 1995). A warming climate resulting in permafrost thawing and enlarged wet surface areas would probably cause increased organic accumulation but also increased methane production in northern taga and tundra (Martikainen, 1996).

The Ushink mite is situated at the southern limit of discontinuous permafrost distribution. It is likely that the predicted climate warming will cause permafrost thawing in this area. This paleecological study contributes to the understanding of the significance of permafrost for the ecology and carbon balance of mires and the sensitivity of permafrost to climate changes. Research on the mite history before and after permafrost aggradation gives information on the base of which the development of this type of mires under conditions of climate warming may be predicted.

As the largest peatland area of the Komi Republic and Europe, the Ushink mite has long attracted scientific and other interests (particularly conservation efforts). Yet, very few publications about this mite are accessible to the international scientific community. In addition to being an enormous peat store, the mire covers oil reserves in its northeastern part. It is an important nesting place for several rare bird species and was protected in 1978, but oil drilling is still possible.

The Ushink mite is first mentioned in Katz (1928) and Tsinzerling (1929). A large-scale peat study, with profiles across the mite, was made by Getmanov (1950), but was not published. Nikonov (1958) studied vegetation and peat deposits. His results are published as part of a report on 'Peat Resources of the Komi Republic', which is considered as classified literature. Preliminary results of permafrost dates in the mite are given by Oksanen (2002).

In the northern and extreme northern taga subzones of Northeast European Russia, mite vegetation studies have been conducted by Katz (1928, 1930), Tsinzerling (1929) and Alekseeva (1972, 1977). Vegetation studies are presented in a generalized form only, e.g., they do not include relev es giving information on small-scale habitats within permafrost mires. Peat-stratigraphic descriptions for Northeast European Russia are reported in Nikonov (1953), P'yavchenko (1955) and Alekseeva (1971, 1974a, b, 1978, 1988). Absolute permafrost aggradation dates are not available in these studies but P'yavchenko (1955) used pollen stratigraphy for indirect dating of permafrost occurrence.

Plant macrofossil studies with radiocarbon dates from mires are reported in Oksanen _et al._ (1998, 2001) from the forest-tundra zone and in Valiranta _et al._ (2003) from the tundra zone. The same methods used in these previous studies are applied to the Ushink mite, in the northern taga, in order to compare the age and dynamics of permafrost in different eco-climatic zones. The main purpose of the present study is to date permafrost aggradation and study its effect on ecology, hydrology and carbon balance of the mite ecosystem. The research is conducted in the central part of the Ushink mite, where permafrost landforms occur. Modern vegetation is studied as a reference for the interpretation of plant macrofossil assemblages. The Holocene vegetation succession and peat accumulation rates are discussed.

## Study Area

The Ushink mite occupies an area of about 1 400 km\({}^{2}\) near the confluence of the rivers Pechora and Usa, Komi Republic, Russia (Fig. 1). Our investigations are limited to an area of about 12 km\({}^{2}\) in the central part of the mite (Fig. 2). The coordinates for the investigated area are approximately 65\({}^{\circ}\) 45\({}^{\circ}\) N and 57\({}^{\circ}\) 30\({}^{\circ}\) E; the elevation is about 50 m asl.

The study area belongs to the Timan-Pechora region, which is a post-Bakalian (Lower Paleozoic) platform (Khain, 1985). At Ushinsk, Jurassic and Cretaceous clays, sands and sandstones are found under Quaternary glacifluvial and glacioalucustrine sediments (Anonymous, 1964). The glacial history of Northeast European Russia is a much debated subject (_e.g._, Gusiltser and Isaichev, 1983; Faustova, 1984; Velichko _et al.,_ 1984; Tvernager, 1995; Astakhov _et al.,_ 1998; Grosswald, 1998). According to most interpretations, the area was glaciated during the Quaternary, possibly the Moscowian glaciation or earlier. The southern limit of the last glaciation, Valdai, was located at least 100 km north of our study area. At some time during Valdai, the area was covered by proglacial Lake Komi, the shorelines of which are found at _ca._ 100 m asl (Faustova, 1984; Lavrov _et al.,_ 1986; Astakhov _et al.,_ 1999; Mangerud _et al.,_ 1999; Svendsen _et al.,_ 1999).

The Ushink mite is located in the extreme northern taga subzone. Forests are mainly composed of _P'icona abies ssp. obovata, Pinus syvestris_ and _Betula pubescens_ (Tolmachev, 1974). The southern limit of discontinuous permafrost in the Pechora lowlands follows approximately the 65\({}^{\circ}\) 30\({}^{\circ}\) N latitude. Permatrost occurs as isolated patches in the form of small peat plateaus, palsas and strings in Aqua mires. The mean annual temperature in the nearest town, Ust-Usa (Fig. 1), for the period 1961-1990, -3.3 \({}^{\circ}\)C, and the mean temperature of the warmest month, July, is 14.8 \({}^{\circ}\)C. The mean total annual precipitation is 487 mm (average for 19 years during 1961-1990). Snow cover lasts 210 days in average, and the average maximum depth of snow in March is 60 cm (Anonymous, 1989). In the town of Pechora (Fig. 1), outside the permafrost region, the mean annual temperature for the period 1961-1990 is -2.4 \({}^{\circ}\)C, and the mean July temperature 16.1 \({}^{\circ}\)C. The mean total annual precipitation is 579 mm (Anonymous, 1996). Snow cover lasts 205 days, andFigure 1: The Pechron Region with the location of the Uusink mixe indicated by the black arrow. Northly mean temperatures and total precipitation for 1961-1990 are from the nearest stations. Climate diagrams are drawn after Walter (1973).

